# Keep Cloud Build source uploads small + fast. The worker Dockerfile runs
# `yarn install` + builds from source, so none of these are needed in the
# upload (and several are huge). MONEY-672 worker deploy.
.git/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.next/
**/.next/
dist/
**/dist/
.turbo/
**/.turbo/
coverage/
**/coverage/
.design-ref/
vendor/
# Claude Code agent worktrees. `.claude/worktrees/<agent>` is a FULL CHECKOUT
# per background agent, and `node_modules/` above only trims the deps — the
# SOURCE still uploads. Measured 2026-09-22 with four agents live: 8,988
# tracked files in the main checkout against 111,672 worktree source files,
# and 21 GB on disk. So a routine `setup-sync-job.sh` (whose image COPYs
# exactly ONE shell script) was uploading four branches of in-progress work to
# the build bucket. Nothing breaks; it is slow and it publishes unmerged code.
.claude/
# Python virtualenvs (MONEY-979). `services/us-fundamentals/.venv` measures
# 578 MiB and is gitignored — but a `.gcloudignore` REPLACES `.gitignore` for
# the source upload rather than adding to it, so anything not listed here is
# uploaded no matter what git thinks. `.next/`, `.turbo/`, `coverage/` and
# `dist/` were already covered; this one arrived with the Python service and was
# not. Note this is a different mechanism from `.dockerignore`, which governs
# the daemon's build context, not what gcloud uploads.
.venv/
**/.venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/*.egg-info/

# Committed TradingView library (~385 MiB) — only the web app serves it; the
# worker never touches it. Excluding it cuts the worker deploy upload massively.
apps/web/public/charting_library/
**/*.log
.DS_Store
